# Inode Exhaustion Investigation & Recovery

## Objective

Simulate and troubleshoot a Linux filesystem inode exhaustion issue where applications report:

```text
No space left on device
```

even though sufficient disk space is available.

The lab demonstrates how to identify inode exhaustion using `df -i`, locate the directory consuming the inodes, recover the filesystem, and validate that file creation works again.

## Scenario

A production application reports:
- File uploads are failing
- New log files cannot be created
- Applications return `No space left on device`

Initial investigation with `df -h` shows plenty of free space. This suggests the problem may be **inode exhaustion**.

## Lab Environment

- OS: Rocky Linux 10.2
- Filesystem: ext4
- Environment: VMware Home Lab
- Filesystem: 100 MB loopback filesystem
- Access: Root

## Phase 1 — Create the Lab Filesystem

Create a 100 MB loopback file:

```bash
dd if=/dev/zero of=/root/inode.img bs=1M count=100
```

(Troubleshooting/image-2.png)

Create an ext4 filesystem with a deliberately low inode count:

```bash
mkfs.ext4 -N 1000 /root/inode.img
```

![alt text](image-3.png)

The filesystem created approximately 1,040 inodes.

Create the mount point:

```bash
mkdir /mnt/inode_lab
```

Mount the filesystem:

```bash
mount -o loop /root/inode.img /mnt/inode_lab
```

![alt text](image-4.png)

Verify:

```bash
df -h /mnt/inode_lab
df -i /mnt/inode_lab
```

## Phase 2 — Simulate an Application

Create an application upload directory:

```bash
mkdir /mnt/inode_lab/uploads
```

Create a test upload:

```bash
touch /mnt/inode_lab/uploads/test1.jpg
```
![alt text](image-5.png)

Verify:

```bash
ls -l /mnt/inode_lab/uploads
```

At this point, file creation works normally.

## Phase 3 — Exhaust the Inodes

Move into the upload directory:

```bash
cd /mnt/inode_lab/uploads
```

Create a large number of small files:

```bash
touch file{1..1500}
```

![alt text](image-6.png)

Eventually the filesystem reports:

```text
No space left on device
```

This is the simulated production incident.

## Phase 4 — Initial Investigation

Check disk space:

```bash
df -h /mnt/inode_lab
```

![alt text](image-7.png)

Example:

```text
Filesystem   Size  Used  Avail  Use%
/dev/loop0    95M   39K   88M    1%
```

There is still approximately **88 MB available**.

Next check inode usage:

```bash
df -i /mnt/inode_lab
```

Example:

```text
Filesystem   Inodes  IUsed  IFree  IUse%
/dev/loop0     1040   1040      0   100%
```

![alt text](image-8.png)

### Root Cause

**The filesystem has exhausted all available inodes.**

## Phase 5 — Reproduce the Failure

Try creating another file:

```bash
touch newfile
```

Result:

```text
touch: cannot touch 'newfile': No space left on device
```

![alt text](image-9.png)

This confirms that new files cannot be created even though disk space is still available.

## Phase 6 — Identify the Problem Directory

Count all files:

```bash
find /mnt/inode_lab -type f | wc -l
```

Check the upload directory:

```bash
find /mnt/inode_lab/uploads -type f | wc -l
```

![alt text](image-10.png)

In this lab, approximately 1,028 files were present in `uploads`.

This identifies the upload directory as the main inode consumer.

## Phase 7 — Investigate File Size

Inspect the files:

```bash
ls -lh | head
```

Example:

```text
-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 0 file1
-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 0 file10
-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 0 file100
```

The files are **0-byte files**.

Although they consume almost no disk space, each file still requires an inode.

Therefore:

```text
Many small files
       ↓
Many inodes consumed
       ↓
Inodes reach 100%
       ↓
New files cannot be created
```

## Phase 8 — Recovery

The generated test files are unnecessary.

Remove them:

```bash
rm -f file*
```

![alt text](image-11.png)

Check inode usage:

```bash
df -i /mnt/inode_lab
```

![alt text](image-12.png)
After cleanup:

```text
Filesystem   Inodes  IUsed  IFree  IUse%
/dev/loop0     1040     13   1027     2%
```

The filesystem now has free inodes again.

## Phase 9 — Validate Recovery

Check disk space:

```bash
df -h /mnt/inode_lab
```

Check inode usage:

```bash
df -i /mnt/inode_lab
```

Create new files:

```bash
touch recovered
touch healthy
```

![alt text](image-13.png)

Verify:

```bash
ls -l recovered healthy test1.jpg
```

File creation succeeds.

### Recovery confirmed.

## Investigation Flow

```text
Application cannot create files
            ↓
"No space left on device"
            ↓
          df -h
            ↓
   Disk space available?
            ↓
           YES
            ↓
          df -i
            ↓
   Inodes at 100%?
            ↓
           YES
            ↓
 Find directory with many files
            ↓
 Identify unnecessary files
            ↓
       Clean up files
            ↓
          df -i
            ↓
 Create test file
            ↓
    Service restored
```

## Key Learnings

### `df -h` vs `df -i`

```text
df -h → Disk space usage
df -i → Inode usage
```

When troubleshooting `No space left on device`, check both.

### Free disk space does not always mean files can be created

A filesystem can have significant free disk space while having **0 free inodes**.

### Every file requires an inode

Even a 0-byte file requires an inode.

### Small-file workloads can cause inode exhaustion

Common examples include:
- Application upload directories
- Cache directories
- Temporary directories
- Mail servers
- Log directories
- CI/CD workspaces
- Container environments

### Increasing disk capacity alone may not solve inode exhaustion

If the filesystem has exhausted its inode supply, simply adding storage does not necessarily provide additional inodes.

Recovery generally involves:
- Removing unnecessary files
- Cleaning temporary/cache files
- Identifying application behavior generating excessive files
- Recreating the filesystem with an appropriate inode configuration when required

